cross has a meld of T5-6-7, so T5 is already used. Maybe not.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can look for pairs. I have W6 twice. That's a pair. Then the rest would need to be three melds. Let's see. If I have W6 as a pair, then the other tiles need to form three melds. The W1-2-3 and W4-5-6 would be two chows, and then maybe the B1, B2, B6. Hmm, B1 and B2 could form a chow if I get a B3. But B3 has 4 left. If I discard B6, maybe someone else can take it. Alternatively, maybe I can form a pung of B1 or B2 if I get two more. But I only have one each.\n\nLooking at the valid actions, I can play B2, T3, T4, W4, etc. If I play B2, maybe someone can take it for a chow. But I already have B2 in hand once. Wait, my hand has B2 once. If I play it, others might take it. But if I keep it, maybe I can form a pung if they discard another B2. But there are only 1 B2 left in unshown tiles. Since I have one, and there's one left, if I play it, another player might get it and form a pung. But maybe it's better to keep it for myself.\n\nAlternatively, looking at T4. I have two T4s. If I play T4, maybe someone can take it for a chow. But the player across has T5-6-7, so maybe they can take T4 for a chow if they have T5 and T3. But their meld is T5-6-7, so maybe they don't have T4. Not sure.\n\nWhat about W4? I have one W4. If I play it, someone might take it. But I have W4 in hand once. The unshown W4 has 3 left. So maybe it's safe to play it. But if I keep it, maybe I can form a chow with W3, W4, W5. Since I have W3, W4, W5 each once. If I can keep W4, maybe I can form that chow if I get another W4. But I only have one. Hmm.\n\nLooking at the unshown tiles, W6 has 4 left. I have two W6s. If I can form a pair with W6, and then have other melds. But I need to see if the rest can form melds. Let me think again. If I have W1, W2, W3 (chow), W4, W5, W6 (chow), and a pair of W6, then that's four melds and a pair. Then the remaining tiles would be W9, T3, T4, B1, B2, B6. Wait, that's six tiles.