I can form two chows, like W1-2-3 and W4-5-6. Since I have W6 twice, that could be a pair. Then the remaining tiles would be W9, T3, T4, B1, B2, B6. Hmm, not sure how to handle those. Maybe discard B6 or B1 or B2. But the valid actions don't include those. Wait, the valid actions are Play B2, T3, T4, W4, etc. So maybe playing T4 is a way to reduce the number of tiles and make room for other melds.\n\nAlternatively, playing W4. If I play W4, maybe someone can take it for a chow. But I have W3 and W5, so if I have W3, W4, W5, that's a chow. So playing W4 would mean losing that opportunity. Maybe better to keep W4.\n\nHmm, this is tricky. Let me check the scoring patterns. Since my hand is concealed, maybe I can aim for a Fully Concealed Hand (4 points). Also, if I can form four chows and a pair, that would be All Chows (2 points). But I need to see if possible.\n\nIf I can form two chows with W1-2-3 and W4-5-6, plus another chow. Let's see. The remaining tiles would be W9, T3, T4, B1, B2, B6. Maybe T3-4-5 if I can get a T5. But T5 is already in a meld. So not possible. Alternatively, B1-2-3 if I can get a B3. B3 has 4 left. If I can get a B3, then that's a chow. But I need to discard to make room. Maybe discard B6. But B6 is in valid actions? No, the valid actions don't include B6. The valid actions are Play B2, T3, T4, W4, etc. So I can't discard B6 unless it's in the valid actions. Wait, the valid actions listed are: [Play B2; Play T3; Play T4; Play W4; Play W5; Play B1; Play B6; Play W1; Play W3; Play W2; Play W9; Play W6]. Oh, B6 is a valid action. So I can play B6. If I play B6, maybe someone can take it for a chow. But I have only one B6. The unshown B6 has 4 left. So playing B6 might not help much.\n\nAlternatively, if I play T4, which is a valid action. Let's say I play T4. Then, if someone can take it for a chow, but if not, I can keep the other tiles. Maybe this is the best move to reduce my hand and increase chances of forming melds later.\n", "output": "Evaluation: [Considering the concealed hand and potential for forming multiple chows, discarding T4 may allow forming a chow with T3 and T5 if available, while maintaining flexibility in the W suit.]\nReason: [Discarding T4 could help form a chow with T3 and T5 if available, and the W suit has potential for multiple chows (W1-2-3, W4-5-6). Keeping W4 for a possible chow with W3 and W5 is beneficial. Fully concealed hand and potential All Chows scoring are advantageous.]\nAnswer: Play T4"}
